2. WHAT EACH ARM COST

   Measured Vina-subprocess wall time, summed over the same 303 complexes
   the gates use.

   arm     threads  wall h  median s  trim-mean s  ×prev  machine  core h
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   exh18        32   2.137      16.9         19.3      —    +1.7%    68.4
   exh32        20   2.556      19.6         23.4  1.415        —    51.1
   exh64        32   3.141      24.1         28.3  1.293    -8.9%   100.5
   exh92        32   4.842      33.1         39.7  1.367    +9.8%   154.9
   exh128       32   4.732      33.4         40.9  1.064    -1.7%   151.4

   'median s' and 'trim-mean s' are per complex. '×prev' is the paired per-complex
   median ratio to the rung below — a robust step size that a single slow complex
   cannot invert, unlike the total.

   'machine' is the residual arm effect on log wall time after removing the per-complex
   effect and the fitted exhaustiveness trend — the part of an arm's cost its search
   effort does not explain. Spread across the ladder: 18.7 percentage points.

   THIS IS THE DOMINANT LIMITATION OF THE COST SIDE. Each arm is one batch, run on
   one day, with no replication and no background-load telemetry. The batch effect is
   the same size as the treatment effect and is perfectly aliased with it. No cost
   number here separates 'exhaustiveness 92 is expensive' from 'the machine was busy
   on the afternoon exhaustiveness 92 ran'.

   COST INVERSION — the ladder is not monotone in measured wall time:
     exh92 → exh128: total -0.110 wall-h, yet 186 of 303 complexes are
     individually SLOWER at exh128 (sign test p<1e-4, Wilcoxon p=5.8e-04) and the paired per-complex median ratio
     is 1.064. Dropping the 3 largest single contributors turns the total to +0.088 wall-h.
     The sum inverts; the location does not. A few complexes that ran anomalously slowly
     in the exh92 batch carry it.

     No return-per-hour is computed across an inverted segment. The cost delta is not a
     cost, and dividing by it would produce a confident number with no referent. This is
     the batch confound above, showing up as an arithmetic impossibility rather than as
     a subtle bias — the same algorithm cannot get cheaper by searching harder.

   Core-seconds are reported for reference only. They are wall x cpus_per_worker, i.e.
   a constant rescale for every arm sharing the modal thread allocation and a different
   one for any arm that does not. On that basis the first marginal cost of this ladder
   is NEGATIVE, which is multiplier arithmetic, not a measurement. No marginal return
   in this report uses core-seconds.

   Wall time also grows far more slowly than exhaustiveness (log-log slope 0.39 against 1.0 for proportional),
   because the Monte-Carlo runs are spread over the thread pool and a large part of each
   whole-protein complex's cost is exhaustiveness-independent setup. Wall time at a fixed
   allocation therefore FLATTERS high exhaustiveness relative to the compute it consumes.
   Exhaustiveness is never used as a proxy for cost here, in either direction.

