   Δcplx is a NET difference; 'gain/loss' shows the two directions separately, because
   at rank-1 the top step both gains and loses complexes and they partly cancel.
   The interval is a paired complex-level bootstrap that recomputes the cost on the SAME
   resampled complexes — holding it fixed would understate the variance and break the
   pairing, since the complexes extra sampling rescues are the slow ones.

   'per extra cplx' is the wall time bought per additional recovered complex. It refuses
   rather than printing a number when the yield delta it divides by is not reliably
   positive. Where it does print, it is a plug-in quotient (Δ wall-h / Δcplx) carrying NO
   interval: its denominator has mass near zero, so a percentile interval on it would not
   be an interval. The bootstrap decides whether the cell prints, not what it prints, so
   conservative claims should rest on complexes-per-hour rather than on its reciprocal.

6. DO RETURNS DIMINISH?

   gate     depth               first cplx/h  last cplx/h  contrast             95% CI  diminishing  family      
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   RMSD≤2Å  rank-1                    +40.56        +0.00    +40.30   [+19.63, +86.53]  YES          descriptive 
   RMSD≤2Å  oracle (all poses)        +66.81        +4.12    +62.47  [+30.38, +134.21]  YES          descriptive 
   triple   rank-1                    +28.63        -0.59    +29.13   [+12.67, +62.95]  YES          descriptive 
   triple   oracle (all poses)        +66.81        +3.53    +62.43  [+31.96, +133.26]  YES          confirmatory

   The contrast is (return per hour on exh18 → exh32) minus (return per hour on exh64 → exh92),
   both recomputed on the SAME resampled complexes. A CI above zero is the diminishing-
   returns claim.
   The two segments are disjoint on this ladder, so the pairing is at the COMPLEX level
   only and the interval is effectively the independent-segments interval. No shared-rung
   term widens it.

   No minimum detectable difference, sample-size argument or type-I simulation exists
   for this statistic. Its SIGN and interval are the claim; its MAGNITUDE moves with the
   cost basis and should never be quoted without naming that basis.

